  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday Night through Sunday)\nIssued at 1146 PM CDT Sun Jun 14 2026\n\nHigh pressure over the region departs Tuesday, with low level \nsoutherly flow returning to the CWA. In response, a warming trend \nsets up for the middle part of the week along with a more humid \nairmass over the region. The warmest day of the week is forecast \nWednesday, with high temperatures climbing back into the upper \n80s to mid 90s and heat index values of mid 90s to near 105 \ndegrees. If these conditions verify, a Heat Advisory may be needed\nfor parts of northeast Oklahoma Wednesday afternoon. \n\nNorthwesterly upper level flow for the first half of the week will \nallow for a shortwave to drop southeast through the northern \nPlains and Midwest Tuesday. A trailing frontal boundary associated\nwith the wave is progged to move through the central Plains and \ncould become the focus for convective development Tuesday \nafternoon into Tuesday night. Latest model solutions continue to \nhighlight the potential for convection to form into a MCS and move\ntoward/into the CWA. The greater potential for storms is \ncurrently over northeast Oklahoma into far northwest Arkansas into\nTuesday night with an increasing low level jet. A limited severe \npotential exists for strong winds, however weakening instability \nand the majority of the low level jet remaining north of the CWA \ncould limit the overall potential. \n\nThe more likely chance of showers and thunderstorms is forecast \nWednesday night into Friday with another shortwave and associated \ncold front moving into/through the Plains. There also continues to \nbe indications of a wave lifting northward out of Mexico into the \nsouthern Plains late week which would add to the moisture content \nover the CWA. As of this forecast, there remains uncertainty with \nthe timing and track of this southern wave as the GFS is faster and \nfarther north compared to the ECMWF. Either way with the front \nmoving into the region, shower and storm chances are forecast for \nthe latter half of the week. During the weekend, there is potential \nfor the boundary to lift back northward through the CWA and generate \nadditional storm chances through Sunday. Limited severe potentials \ncould develop ahead of the front Thursday into Friday and again \nbehind the front as it lifts back northward. With the multiple \nchances for precip, a heavy rain threat also exists through the \nweekend. Temperatures late week into the week look to be near to \nslightly below the seasonal average.",
